{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/979398-signs-perfected-metal-equipment","id":979398,"title":"[Signs] Perfected Metal Equipment","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-09-30T18:37:55-07:00","id":979398,"title":"[Signs] Perfected Metal Equipment","content":"Obviously, this is subject to change, but:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> An item created with a Perfected metal still functions at its mundane counterpart, though it is created from partially-Supernal material. It has the same properties and equipment bonuses as any other mundane object of the same kind. </div> </div> </div>It strikes me that this cannot possibly be true. Take Siderite in particular, which explicitly mentions that &quot;It can cut through anything, ignoring the Durability of an object.&quot; This is a specific refutation of the above statement - or perhaps a modification of it? There's also apeiron's 8-again bonus for mundane tool use (which strikes me as a really interesting image since it can be shaped by hand. I'd imagine it's like one of those high-viscosity liquids, where it's quite pliable unless hit with a sudden strike.)<br /> <br /> The description strikes me as roughly the difference between a steel sword and a copper sword, if not much more. What effect would a Siderite sword have on regular old steel armor? Or on flesh, for that matter? If it can cut through anything, it would penetrate flesh and bone with minimal resistance, and that strikes me as deserving of an equipment bonus for damage. Would it ignore armor ratings entirely for mundane protection?<br /> <br /> What about Siderite armor? It's noticeably immune to heating, and presumably will not yield to strikes from common weapons. It resists being hammered into shape without repeated cycles of heating/cooling. That's an armor bonus in all but name - if, say, a demon with a giant hammer struck you right in the center of a siderite breastplate, obviously, some amount of force is going to impact you, but only from the sudden acceleration of the plate into your body (and potentially the impact of your body against the wall behind you.) Such a plate would almost certainly be fully proof against bullets (though it'd be like being struck with hammers still!)<br /> <br /> I don't bring this up to complain, but to ask: what <i>should</i> expected equipment bonuses be for the various perfected metals? Obviously this could vary dramatically. A bulletproof vest of kassiterum fibers and a full medieval suit of kassiterum plate will both have very different properties, but both of them are bound to be superior to steel and maybe kevlar (admittedly, kevlar is pretty damned strong - 5 times the tensile strength of steel, which might be comparable to kassiterum.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>We don't allow mages to cast spells, since this is the most unbalancing rule of all.","edited":{"userid":5226,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","user":"Jachra","date":". 09-30-2016, 09:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5226-jachra","name":"Jachra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"265"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}